From 457d21b8e8be36232f213e623ed41e6caf2b4921 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:32:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] microblaze: rewrite the pcmpbf strlen in assembly

With pattern compare, strlen in libc/machine/microblaze/strlen.c is a
whole function body in one asm statement inside a C function.  It
reads its argument from r5, returns with its own rtsd, clobbers r3,
r4, r9 and r10 without declaring them, and defines global labels.  It
works only because GCC gives the function no frame.  It also throws
away the result of pcmpbf, the position of the NUL in the word, and
scans the word again a byte at a time.

Move it to strlen-pcmp.S and take the length from pcmpbf.  pcmpbf
counts byte positions from the most significant byte, and lw on
big-endian and lwr on little-endian both load the lowest-addressed
byte there, so the NUL is at the word's address plus the position
minus one in both byte orders.  lwr is what the old code used on
little-endian too.  The head is still a byte loop up to the first word
boundary, so no load reaches a word that holds no byte of the string.

strlen.c keeps the C version for cores without pattern compare.  Both
files now test __HAVE_HW_PCMP__, so exactly one defines strlen.  GCC
defines HAVE_HW_PCMP, which the old code tested, only outside strict
ISO modes.

Under QEMU, at every alignment 0-7 and length 0-160, both byte orders,
both versions return the right length.  For alignments 0-3 and lengths
0-160 the new code executes 65316 instructions to the old code's
81855, and it is 16 instructions long instead of 23.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
